 ### [PSF-based Multiple Scattering Rendering](1297-psf-based-multiple-scattering-rendering.md)
-PSF-based Multiple Scattering Rendering is a practical method for rendering volumetric multiple scattering effects by treating the participating medium as a point spread function (PSF) that blurs incident radiance, thereby avoiding the costly direct numerical simulation of the radiative transfer equation. Unlike diffusion approximation, which is valid only for optically dense homogeneous media and assumes uniform angular distribution, this method handles spatially varying general materials and highly directional effects. Choose it for spatially varying media with directional lighting where diffusion fails.
+PSF-based Multiple Scattering Rendering is a practical method for rendering volumetric multiple scattering effects by treating the participating medium as a point spread function (PSF) that blurs incident radiance, thereby avoiding the costly direct numerical simulation of the radiative transfer equation. Unlike diffusion approximation, which is valid only for optically dense homogeneous media and assumes uniform angular distribution, this method handles spatially varying general materials and highly directional effects. Unlike the method by Narasimhan and Nayar, which is restricted to homogeneous isotropic media, this rendering algorithm allows for spatially varying participating media and accounts for anisotropic sources and visibility effects. Choose it for spatially varying media with directional lighting where diffusion fails.
 
