 ### [Precomputed Multiple Scattering Table](3324-precomputed-multiple-scattering-table.md)
-Multiple scattering corresponds to light that has reached $v_i$, is scattered several times before reaching $P_k$ and is scattered one last time into direction $\hat{o}$. It explicitly stores directional lobes of outgoing radiance rather than just scalar intensity or width parameters. Choose it when rotational symmetry of an infinite medium can be exploited and directional accuracy is prioritized over spatially varying lookup overhead.
+This method approximates the contribution of light that has undergone multiple scattering events within a homogeneous participating medium by storing the material's radiance response in a precomputed lookup table. It solves the computational cost of simulating high-order scattering in real-time or near-real-time rendering by exploiting the rotational symmetry of an infinite medium and normalizing distances by the mean free path. It explicitly stores directional lobes of outgoing radiance rather than just scalar intensity or width parameters, and it serves as the primary multiple scattering term before optional corrections like Bounced Multiple Scattering are applied. Choose it when rotational symmetry of an infinite medium can be exploited and directional accuracy is prioritized over spatially varying lookup overhead.
 
 ### [One-pass Spectral Rendering Integral](5265-one-pass-spectral-rendering-integral.md)
-Bergner et al. [7, 8] developed a spectral volume rendering integral based on a simplified multiple scattering model. It removes global illumination elements to facilitate ray casting with local illumination and post-illumination with different light sources. Choose it when spectral accuracy and interactive post-illumination are required, decoupling light source intensity from volumetric transport.
+The One-pass Spectral Rendering Integral is a real-time approximation for light transport in participating media that factors out the light source to enable interactive post-illumination. It simplifies the multiple scattering model by removing global illumination elements, allowing ray casting with local illumination and subsequent application of different light sources. It is distinguished from its siblings by its specific spectral formulation and the decoupling of light source intensity $L(\lambda)$ from the volumetric transport term $\tilde{I}_i$, which is not present in the non-spectral siblings. Choose it when spectral accuracy and interactive post-illumination are required.
